'''Self-organized criticality''' (SOC) describes a proposed class of dynamical systems that evolve toward critical-like, scale-free behavior without precise external tuning of a control parameter.

== Canonical sandpile model ==
Bak, Tang and Wiesenfeld (1987, 1988) proposed SOC to explain scale-invariant correlations in driven dissipative systems. On a finite square lattice, let h_i be an integer height. Add one grain at a random site after the previous avalanche stops. If h_i≥4, subtract four and send one grain to each nearest neighbor; grains leaving open boundaries are lost. Repeat until all sites are stable. Drive and relaxation are separated in time, and the boundary provides dissipation.

<pre>
repeat for N additions:
  choose a site uniformly; increase its height by 1
  while any site has height >= 4:
    topple that site; distribute grains to neighbors
    discard grains crossing the boundary
  record number of topplings and duration under a defined schedule
</pre>

== Scaling and model tests ==
A candidate avalanche-size distribution may have a form <math>P(s;L)\sim s^{-\tau}F(s/L^D)</math>, where L is system size and F represents a cutoff. Estimating τ requires a specified range, uncertainty, goodness-of-fit and comparison with alternatives such as lognormal or truncated distributions. Apparent straight lines on log-log plots are insufficient evidence. Check finite-size scaling, time-scale separation and stationarity, not just the tail shape.

== Distinctions and limitations ==
Self-organization is a broader concept, and emergence need not involve criticality. The behavior of real granular material depends on friction, particle shapes and drive. Experiments and models can deviate from SOC; a power law alone does not establish its mechanism. Apply the framework to neural, ecological or social processes only when system dynamics and competing explanations are independently assessed.

